00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.420
Why do you need to build
modern applications

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.540
using an API-first strategy?

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.950
And the simple answer is the
whole aspect of modernization

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.330
is such a huge driver.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.420
We have to modernize every
aspect of our life, right?

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.300
We are modernizing
our phones every year.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.790
We are modernizing
our smart homes

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.560
and all aspects of our life.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.610
And this modernization
also obviously trickles

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:33.807
down into every aspect of
an enterprise or a startup,

00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:34.640
whoever it might be.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.450
If you look at the IT landscape,
the software landscape,

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.530
that is changing a lot.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.490
Normally, over the
last 10, 15, 20 years,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.270
we would talk about our legacy
architectures, our websites,

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.710
mobile apps, partners.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.080
Even mobile is
pretty new, right?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.510
Mobile is maybe in
the last 5, 10 years,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.220
when it became mainstream.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.470
But that itself is
changing drastically.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.540
If you look at where the
world is going towards,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.510
we are talking about IoTs
and connected platforms,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.860
like connected cars,
omnichannel strategies.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.970
The backend systems are
moving more and more

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.560
towards microservices.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.700
We are talking about chat bots
and AI-based bots, machine

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.610
learning.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.480
We are talking about various
kinds of cloud integrations,

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.870
on how to integrate across
multiple clouds and SaaS

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.720
applications, how to
migrate your existing

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.840
applications from your
on-prem data centers to cloud,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.630
and how to build
hybrid architectures.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.210
So it is a drastically
different landscape

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:38.580
than what you have today.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.310
A lot of this also is
delivered through APIs.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.310
Keep in mind, when you
want to expose your data,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.770
to the connected cars or to
your IoTs or to you chat bots,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.800
there is an API
behind all of that.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.320
The APIs themselves are
changing, as well, right?

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.120
The designs of the
APIs are changing.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.760
When we first started
seriously thinking about APIs,

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.540
first there was RPC-style APIs
way back in the early '90s.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.460
SOAP became really popular
in mid-2000s and all.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.210
And then, now the world
has, more or less,

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:09.170
moved towards REST.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.300
But there are other
forms of APIs,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.260
like oDATA or GraphQL
or WebSockets or GRPC.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.470
And there are so many
different just design patterns

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.530
of APIs, which are existent,
and that's a constantly evolving

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.260
landscape.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.350
The security of those
APIs also changes.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:31.370
So we used to have SAML
or PLS-based or IP-based

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.700
whitelisting or things
like NTLM, Kerberos

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.050
in the past, which
has mostly given way

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.120
to things like OAuth
or OpenID Connect

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.700
or JWE or even basic auth.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.050
So what happens is, as new and
newer technologies come up,

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.850
some part of your stack adopts.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.030
Let's say some part will
adopt JWE or OpenID Connect.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.620
But you will have
some other stack,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.580
which is still running on
SAML or running on mutual TLS.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.550
There's even a
brand new proposal

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.040
for a security
standard called SPIFFE,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.500
which came out last year.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.430
It's an open source
standard from Google

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.230
for managing service-to-service
communications

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.720
in a microservices world.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.960
So this is a constantly
evolving landscape,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.820
in terms of API security.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.160
The other thing which
changes are the consumers.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.560
Previously, all the APIs,
which you used to have,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.850
used to go and either serve
internal applications or maybe

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:30.370
a website or maybe one
web-optimized mobile app.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:33.300
But now, you are building
native applications in Android.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.660
You are building various kinds
of connected experiences.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.460
You are building iOS apps,
IoT apps, smart homes,

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.270
all sorts of different
consumers who

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.750
these APIs are serving up to.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.000
So all this change
inevitably results

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.880
in where you have an
IT infrastructure where

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.630
you have to manage a number
of different versions

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:00.440
of your APIs.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.800
So you might have
some SOAP, some REST.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.550
You have a few different
versions of REST.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.210
You might have some
GraphQL APIs and whatnot.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.730
You have different types
of security protocols.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.400
You might have
different API standards,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.520
which internally leads
to inefficient developer

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:17.660
on-boardings, right?

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.990
Because you need to onboard
more and more developers

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:25.420
from internal, different
parts of the organization,

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.220
or from your partners, or
the long tail of developers,

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.930
because you need
different developers

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.880
with different key
skill sets, right?

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.760
As we are going to
more and more different

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.320
discrete backgrounds,
different styles of APIs,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.690
how do you onboard these
different developers?

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.110
How do you onboard the different
apps onto your platform?

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.090
And how do you release
faster and better--

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.390
the new versions of those apps?

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.370
How do you manage the
lifecycle of the keys?

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.930
Because, as you have
different consumers,

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.660
you need to have a much
better control on when

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.960
a certain key can be
invalidated, or, when someone

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:06.170
is not following your TNC, you
can mark that as a red flag.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.726
And all of these challenges also
results in a massive challenge

00:06:09.726 --> 00:06:10.850
with the overall analytics.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:14.810
How do you measure all the
usage, errors, latency,

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:15.584
adoption?

00:06:15.584 --> 00:06:17.000
Because you need
to measure things

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.050
from an operational
standpoint, right?

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.950
Across all the different APIs,
how is your average error rate,

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.030
or what's your average latency?

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.490
But you also need
to understand it

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.698
from the business standpoint.

00:06:29.698 --> 00:06:32.440
How is your adoption of
the brand new API, which

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.320
you just published, or the
brand new version of the API?

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.090
And you need to understand
all the usage and adoption.

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.510
How is a specific
type of API getting

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.402
more used in a
mobile app context

00:06:44.402 --> 00:06:45.860
than something
else, which might be

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.945
more popular with IoT devices?

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.630
Now, we have looked at
some of the problems

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:57.640
because of this dynamic,
changing landscape, which

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.360
your backend is changing
and your APIs are changing

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.250
and your consumers are changing.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.881
Now comes the obvious question.

00:07:04.881 --> 00:07:06.810
As part of this
changing landscape,

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.180
how can Apigee play a
role and how can Apigee

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.370
help you evolve faster?

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:17.630
So at a very nutshell, Apigee
helps by creating a platform.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.780
So if you look at
the bottom-half,

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.690
that's where you have your
Google Cloud platform.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.240
You have, maybe,
some other clouds,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.720
maybe some
on-premises-based system.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.260
And these are all
running a bunch

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.240
of systems, which are
exposing data, which

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.200
needs to go up to different
web and Android and iOS

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.590
and a different set of
consumers, essentially.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:43.610
So what's a platform?

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.350
A platform is a
place where you can

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.000
bring these different
technologies

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.670
and the human aspects
of the technologies--

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:52.190
like the developers who are
building those backend systems,

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.980
the developers who are
consuming those backend systems,

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.290
the developers who are
then building the apps,

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:59.450
and, in the end,
users of the apps--

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.460
you can bring all
of them together.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.420
So Apigee can help you, in
your journey of modernization,

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:09.330
by bringing all these
different entities together.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.560
So in the Apigee platform, which
sits, as a facade or a proxy,

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.430
on top of your backend systems,
Apigee will take care of things

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:21.230
like providing you all
the right analytics,

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.860
from operational analytics
to infrastructure analytics

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.360
to business analytics.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.090
It will help you manage all
the developers, the lifecycle

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.920
of the developers,
from the developers

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.440
who are producing the
APIs, to the developers who

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.570
are consuming those, as well
as all aspects of security,

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.929
translation, debugging, and
a whole bunch of things.

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:41.570
And we'll see that
a little later.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:44.450
But the key aspect is
Apigee, as a platform,

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.240
helps you tie back together
all the technologies,

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.110
both in the production side, as
well as the consumption side,

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.757
as well as all the
different human elements

00:08:53.757 --> 00:08:54.465
in this platform.

00:08:54.465 --> 00:08:58.560
It helps you tie it
all back together.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.140
Now, oftentimes, when we
are talking about this,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.660
we get asked questions that,
hey, I would like to go there,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:07.440
where we create this platform.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.350
But over the years, I
mainly have built monoliths,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.170
or that's what we
are left with, right?

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:18.150
So in a monolith world, you
would typically have all these

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:21.360
different capabilities-- like
authentication, authorization,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.770
business rules, et cetera--

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:26.670
all confined in the boundaries
of the same application, which

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.100
are then consumed
by different users.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.720
That's a very, very traditional
landscape in an IT world.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.400
Now, people inherently
understand the problem

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.820
with a monolith like that.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.560
So early 2000s,
the world of SOA--

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.230
Service-Oriented Architecture--
that gradually became popular

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:50.486
and became mainstream.

00:09:50.486 --> 00:09:52.110
And that's when people
started to break

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:56.070
those individual components
into individual services.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.230
We are still talking
about, primarily,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.080
SOAP, with maybe some
SAML-based security

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.790
or certificate-based security.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.850
But this is how, for
many of us, that's

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.340
how the architecture
looks like and not really

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.550
a very traditional monolith.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.670
Now, as the world evolved--

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.080
it's 2018 for everyone--

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.990
we are no longer investing
new into SOA services or SAML

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.280
or SOAP or things like that.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.540
Rather, the new keyword, the
new buzzword is microservices.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:29.220
How can you build things
which are even smaller

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.220
and are easier to manage?

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:35.900
And we can leverage things,
like containers and Kubernetes

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.010
and stuff like
that, to grow them,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:41.100
to scale them, in a hybrid
environment, either Cloud

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.450
or on-prem or
multi-cloud or whatnot.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:46.260
So now, we have a
lot of microservices

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.370
as part of everyday stacks.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.670
If you look at how enterprises
typically look today,

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.000
you will probably see a
combination of a whole bunch

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.240
of different things.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.800
So lets start with the most
obvious on the right-hand side,

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.170
where you can see Cloud Foundry
and Docker and bare-metals

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:05.250
and VMs.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.580
That's your traditional
on-premise environment,

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.460
when you have a data
center or a colo facility,

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.700
where you are either
running bare-metals or VMs

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.850
or running containers,
like Docker,

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.720
or doing other kind
of on-prem software.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.760
From there, some
enterprises have

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.730
evolved to run Private
Cloud, where you are doing

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.530
not only Docker,
where you are actually

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.530
trying to run Cloud Foundry
and Kubernetes on-prem

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.920
and trying to create a private
cloud in your data center

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.830
or in your colo facilities.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.060
Now, the next thing
is Public Cloud.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.330
So you will probably
be using a whole lot

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:47.460
of GCP, maybe some other public
clouds in a hybrid environment.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.950
And then, you have
the SaaS, where

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:53.670
you might be using things like
G Suite and using their APIs.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:57.930
So the key takeaway is this is
a reality for every enterprise,

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.975
whether you are Fortune
2000 organization, who

00:12:00.975 --> 00:12:03.240
has been in business
for 100 years,

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:07.050
or whether you are a startup,
who just started out of Silicon

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:11.210
Valley over the last few years.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:14.580
The systems from where you're
consuming data and the systems

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.420
to where you are exposing
those data, that is becoming

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:22.140
more and more diverse.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:27.130
So let's move on to--

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.420
now, oftentimes, when
you talk about this,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.280
we get the feedback
that, hey, this

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.820
is not what our backend
really looks like.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.950
This is probably where
we would want to go,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.520
but this is not
what it looks like.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.020
And that's the question,
which we get, oftentimes,

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.810
that my architecture does
not look like that today.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:47.400
It might be true for some
parts of your business.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:48.840
Maybe for some applications.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:50.530
It may not be others.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:53.360
But that's totally fine if
your architecture does not

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.380
look like that.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.940
And that's totally fine.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.560
Because, most likely, your
architecture looks like this.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.980
So we talked about
this monolith,

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:08.560
and how monolith has been
a mainstay in enterprises

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.430
for many, many, many years.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:13.180
So this is probably how
your application looks like.

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:14.410
You have a monolith.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.330
In this case, we
are talking about,

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:18.580
let's say, a retail
experience, where

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.290
you have a monolith
application, which

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:24.190
has search, shopping cart,
typeahead, recommendations,

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:24.880
CMS.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:27.100
And all these
capabilities are running

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.975
inside your data center.

00:13:28.975 --> 00:13:31.350
These are exposing all these
capabilities, which are then

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:36.280
consumed by different web
applications or Android or iOS

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:37.220
applications.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:41.020
So monolith exposing some
APIs, which are then consumed

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:43.490
by those web, Android, iOS.

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:46.480
Oftentimes, you
probably don't even

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:50.980
know the existence of the
APIs because of two reasons.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:53.620
Either the monolith, down
below, exposes your SDKs,

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.010
and the SDK, in turn,
hides those APIs.

00:13:57.010 --> 00:14:01.000
Or maybe the monolith, itself,
provides you a direct hook

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.420
into the web, Android, iOS.

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:04.920
It's the same team
who builds those.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.970
And there is a very
deep integration

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:10.630
between those consumer-facing
applications and the backend.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.870
But the challenge is
that now the front end

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:24.470
and the backend, those are
very tightly integrated.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:28.240
So what we need to do is we need
to create a proxy or a facade.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:32.380
We need to separate
those two things out.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.130
And that's where Apigee fits in.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:36.550
Apigee acts as a proxy
or a facade, which

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.700
sits in between that
backend layer, where

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:41.779
you have the monolith, and
you have the frontend layer.

00:14:41.779 --> 00:14:43.320
So first and foremost,
this gives you

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.760
a tremendous flexibility
on the frontend team

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.610
to do things differently,
at a different pace,

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:52.010
than what would
happen at the backend.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:56.120
Now, let's look into how will
that happen in real life.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:57.910
So let's say you
have this monolith.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:59.690
And now the backend
team, they decided

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:02.690
that we need to break
down this monolith.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:06.190
We want to create a
few microservices.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:09.170
Let's say we want to break down
the search and the typeahead,

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:12.320
and write some new services,
maybe property lookup

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:15.200
and whatnot, which is not
available today as part

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:16.700
of the monolith backend.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:20.210
And now they are writing
this thing in microservices,

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:23.750
in GKE, running on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:28.010
Now, because Apigee is
sitting, as an obstruction

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:32.090
layer in front of this backend,
while the backend teams are

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.970
making those changes, they
are moving the services

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:37.370
to Google Cloud
Platform, which takes

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:40.800
a long time, which takes maybe
six months, a year, two years.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:43.010
The frontend teams,
they are not impacted,

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:45.240
because they are getting,
still, those same APIs.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:49.730
And Apigee is taking care of
translating them, mediating

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:50.390
them.

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:54.234
So when the Google
Cloud Platform-based--

00:15:54.234 --> 00:15:56.150
and there's where the
microservices are built,

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:59.810
so the APIs might be
exposed as JSON over REST,

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:01.910
whereas the monolith
might expose SOAP.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:05.000
Apigee will make sure that
what the Android and the web

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:10.070
and the iOS team see is single,
uniform APIs, irrespective

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:12.040
of what the backend is.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:13.340
It's the same security.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:14.900
It has the same
versioning structure.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:18.890
It has the same, let's say, the
layer of caching and everything

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.740
else, like the backend,
whether it is in a monolith

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:23.030
or whether it is in GCP.

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:30.400
So what it leads to
is the frontend team

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:31.480
now gets isolated.

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.960
As you can see, we can
now create one nice box

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:37.630
around those different
frontend teams.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:39.310
Because you might
have a different team

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:40.893
for web, a different
team for Android,

00:16:40.893 --> 00:16:43.270
a different team for iOS.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:45.700
And now there is
a nice box, where

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:50.080
they can work themselves
at an arm's length

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:51.310
from the backend team.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.370
So we are now trying to
break away from the paradigm

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:56.170
that the backend and
the frontend teams

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.270
are very closely
knit together, where

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:02.460
every feature on the frontend
results in a deep code

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:04.089
change at the backend.

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:05.589
Like where we have
seen things like,

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:08.140
if you want to build a new
feature in an Android app,

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:11.440
it might result in
having a stored procedure

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.190
change at your database level.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:15.339
That's obviously not
ideal, and it slows down

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:19.950
the whole process.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:22.160
So now, what you
can see is, maybe

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:27.700
as part of this architecture,
what the retail customer wants

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.920
to do is they want
to move everything

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.850
onto GCP as different services.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:35.900
And they want to keep,
in their private cloud,

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:38.400
or in the data center, they
want to keep their shopping cart

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.650
application.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:43.590
Because it doesn't make sense to
rewrite that as a microservice

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.050
or whatnot.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:47.390
While they're doing
that-- as you can imagine,

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:48.770
this is a massive change.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.670
They are doing
massive reworking,

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.300
and it is happening
over years, maybe.

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:56.520
Instead of the frontend
team being slowed down--

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.186
where they have been
asked that, hey, we

00:17:58.186 --> 00:18:00.420
are making these massive
changes at the back end,

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.700
please make your changes slower.

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:08.940
Instead of that, they actually
added brand new capabilities.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.150
They might have added a new
Google Home integration,

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:15.690
or they might have added
some other native mobile apps

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:17.760
or some new web apps.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:22.350
So the beauty with
Apigee is you can

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.190
create this facade
or a proxy layer,

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:31.910
where you can have the frontend
teams building new apps faster,

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:34.470
going through faster
release cycles of the apps,

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:38.970
while your backend goes through
these tremendous transformative

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:40.950
changes, where you are
shifting your backend,

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.620
either from monoliths
and taking it

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.590
to a lift-and-shift of cloud,
or building out a hybrid cloud,

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:50.260
or building out microservices,
breaking out of your monolith

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:51.660
into microservices.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.990
Whatever shape or form
those changes might be,

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:58.764
Apigee will help you isolate
those changes at the backend.

00:18:58.764 --> 00:19:00.180
And your frontend
teams will still

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:03.930
be able to innovate at a lot
faster pace than probably

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:05.100
where they were before.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.770
Now, how Apigee does that?

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.980
This diagram talks
about something

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:18.020
which is more commonly
known as a two-speed IT,

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:19.780
or a multi-modal IT.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:22.950
These are the words which have
been thrown around in the IT

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:25.830
world in the last few years.

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:26.900
So what does it mean?

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:30.280
Down below, where you see
the CRM and the financials,

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:32.460
HR, supply chain,
and all of that,

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:33.860
that's the system of records.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:37.190
That's the layer which you
hardly ever want to touch.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:40.390
You don't want to go and
change your users' database

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:45.290
schema every few days or
even every few months.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:48.240
Maybe that changes once
every couple of years.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:52.370
So the pace of change over
there is very, very slow--

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:54.330
a matter of years.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.260
But at the top, you have
your systems of engagement.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:02.900
So this is where you have
your web apps and mobile apps,

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.510
either employees' apps
or consumer-facing apps,

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:07.770
or partnered apps and whatnot.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:11.300
Now, at that layer, you
need to move a lot faster.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.270
You need to move in
a matter of days.

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:16.040
If you have an app
out in Google Play,

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:19.160
and you get a feedback that
something is not right,

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:22.220
you cannot wait for
18 months to fix it.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.200
You cannot even wait for
maybe 18 days to fix it.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:27.380
So that's where Apigee
comes in, with this ability

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:31.130
to manage the security, the
scalability, the analytics,

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:32.570
manage all the developers.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:35.990
Apigee acts as a
cushion, where it takes

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:38.030
those systems of records--

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.770
the slow-moving
layer-- and makes it

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.330
into a system of
engagement, where

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:45.060
you can move a lot faster.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:49.280
So this is an example of how
Apigee fits in, in a Google

00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:50.561
Cloud Platform context.

00:20:50.561 --> 00:20:52.310
So let's say you are
writing your backend.

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:54.360
You have the system
of records down below,

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.720
which are untouched,
like your CRMs

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:59.780
and financials and inventory
and HR and whatnot.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.280
Then you are using Google
Cloud Platform, on top of that,

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:03.710
to do a whole bunch of things.

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:07.260
You might be running Google
Cloud Pub/Sub, logging,

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:11.090
Dataflow, Bigtables, computes,
and things like that.

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:14.670
The Cloud Pub/Sub or the
login or the business logic,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.430
which is running in
App Engine, those

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:18.740
are then all exposed out
to your clients, which

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:21.530
are maybe IoTs or
mobile apps or web apps

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:23.230
or partners or whatnot.

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:25.760
Apigee sits as a facade,
in between these two,

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.650
and makes sure all
the data, which

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.650
is exposed from Google Cloud
Platform, goes through Apigee.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.990
And Apigee takes
care of managing

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:36.570
all the APIs, the security,
the operational aspect,

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:37.220
the analytics.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.920
It creates a developer
portal through which

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:43.280
all the developers
on the client side

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.800
can get access to
those APIs easily.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:49.460
Do the bot protections-- so
if you get attacked by spams,

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:53.550
Apigee has bot
detection built-in.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.060
If you want to even
monetize some of those APIs,

00:21:56.060 --> 00:22:01.210
that capability also is built
in as part of that platform.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:03.530
Now, these are,
capabilities-wise,

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:05.080
what you are looking
at from Apigee.

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:10.780
So if you look at Apigee, the
two key trends on both sides

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.550
are the machine
learning and the AI,

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.860
which we use at many
parts of the platform.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:18.100
Down below is the runtime,
where you have the Enterprise

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:19.510
Gateway and Istio.

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:21.760
Then you have a
mediation engine,

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:24.220
which takes care of
security or transformation,

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:26.890
from SOAP to REST,
or XML to JSON.

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:30.580
You have connectors, in which
you can write in Node or Python

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:31.690
or things like that.

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:32.950
You can build orchestrations.

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.410
When one call comes
in, you split it out,

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:39.190
and a few different calls
go to the southbound.

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:40.930
Then there is all
sorts of analytics,

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:43.600
from business analytics
to operations analytics.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.470
And then you have the
whole developer ecosystem,

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.810
where you have the API catalogs,
you have the client SDKs.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:52.450
You are taking your APIs
and creating products.

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:56.050
So Apigee kind of makes
you treat your APIs

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:58.330
as individual
products, just like you

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:01.510
will treat your every
other go-to-market product.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:04.630
And then, you can create
an API marketplace,

00:23:04.630 --> 00:23:07.600
or you can actually sell those
APIs using the monetization

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:09.280
capabilities.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:13.150
So this is how the API platform
looks like, capabilities-wise,

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.620
at a very high level.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:20.210
We are going to now jump into
a quick demo of everything

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.430
we talked about.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:24.342
I have a very simple setup.

00:23:24.342 --> 00:23:28.580
The demo setup is I have two
different applications, which

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:31.170
is serving some hotel's data.

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:35.210
We got some hotel's
data loaded up into GCP.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.910
And then I have a
Node.js app, which

00:23:37.910 --> 00:23:41.150
is running in Google Compute
Engine, in regular VMs.

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:43.160
And then I have a
Spring Boot app,

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:46.490
which is running in GKE, the
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:48.590
And both of these
apps are exposing

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:51.140
APIs, which are then
proxied through Apigee,

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:53.690
and goes to different consumers.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:55.850
In the Apigee world,
the different consumers

00:23:55.850 --> 00:24:00.710
are represented by apps.

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:05.930
So I have two different apps
which are consuming those APIs.

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:09.170
One interesting thing is we are
using the VM Compute Engine.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.590
That is kind of reminiscent
to a legacy application,

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:16.130
how you would run your
traditional VM-based apps.

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.860
And the Spring Boot app,
which is running in GKE,

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:23.410
is like your modern application.

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:25.670
We also have an Apigee
developer portal.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:30.050
This is where we expose all the
details of those APIs, which

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.990
can then be subscribed
to and then consumed

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:37.640
by the developers, who wants
to build the different apps.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:42.420
With that, let me switch
screen and jump into the demo.

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:52.870
So what you see is a
picture of a nice hotel.

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:55.690
So this is our fictitious
hotel, and this

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:58.740
is where the hotel's
developer portal is.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:01.510
If you are not familiar with
what a developer portal is,

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:03.610
a great example for
you to take a look at

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:06.580
is go to developers.Google.com.

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:11.230
This is one single page
through which Google interacts

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:12.640
with all these developers.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:17.230
And please keep in mind, this
is not only for public use cases

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.810
or for long tail of
developer's use cases.

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:22.510
If you are a large enterprise,
if you are building APIs

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:27.190
internally, and if you have
hundreds of developers--

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:29.170
very similar to this,
like a dev portal--

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:31.210
it's extremely easy to
build this platform,

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:35.700
to build this ecosystem, inside
your enterprise, as well.

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:39.120
So please take a look at
this developers.google.com,

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:40.500
this developer portal.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:44.100
And what Apigee helps you to
do is build a developer portal,

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:47.160
just like that, for
your enterprise,

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:50.790
either a private developer
portal or a semi-private--

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:53.990
means it can be invitation-only
to your partners--

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:57.990
or you can have it a true
public developer portal.

00:25:57.990 --> 00:25:59.760
So this is one such
developer portal,

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.500
which I built for the demo.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.610
We call it Hotels R Us.

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:06.650
And as part of this dev
portal, we have some APIs.

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:10.240
We have some pretty
simple-looking pages.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:15.110
But let's say I have this API
called cmap-demo, cmap-hotels.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.280
And there are a
bunch of hotels APIs.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:20.550
Here, I can see an
API for Get All hotels

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:22.820
or Get a hotel using
a particular hotel

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:26.720
name or reviews or
inventory or whatnot.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.440
Now, what I can do is
I can simply go here,

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:32.740
and I can try out this
API, and I can execute it.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:36.050
And I can see the
request and the response.

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:39.160
Now, this is based on open
APIs, which was previously

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:41.020
known as Swagger.

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:44.680
So when you import a Swagger
definition, or an open API

00:26:44.680 --> 00:26:47.320
definition, into Apigee,
Apigee can automatically

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:52.090
create this dynamic
page for you.

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:54.640
The beauty of this
is, without something

00:26:54.640 --> 00:27:00.460
like this, previously, you
would have to probably request

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:03.220
access for an API, have to
wait for a few days, if not

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:03.790
few months.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:05.650
Someone would have
to give you access.

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:08.260
Documentations, with
maybe a PDF, and then

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:11.440
you take those and then
write some client code.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:16.660
And then only you can make the
first call to your API, right?

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:19.090
This is a process
which would have

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:22.180
taken a long time, a lot of
trial and errors, not easy

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:24.590
to get support, and
things like that.

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:26.800
So now, with a
developer portal, anyone

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.890
can come and can
learn about the API.

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:33.330
At this stage, you are
accessing a sandbox environment.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.470
You will not have access
to the production.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:37.760
When you want to get
access to the production,

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:41.440
then the developers have to go
in and log in right over there,

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:44.620
and he can ask for support
for various things.

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:45.400
They can log in.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:46.975
They can get keys.

00:27:46.975 --> 00:27:50.650
You can then approve those keys
for getting used in production.

00:27:50.650 --> 00:27:53.170
And only when you are certain,
about what kind of app

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:55.420
the developer is building
and things like that,

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:57.820
you can have him use
your production APIs.

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:00.940
And Apigee takes care of
creating the segregation,

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:03.220
between sandbox
and the production,

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:05.213
and how do you do
app promotion, how

00:28:05.213 --> 00:28:08.500
do you certify your developer,
and all that kind of stuff.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:14.230
Now, let's see an Apigee screen.

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:16.380
So this is what we
call the Management UI.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:20.970
So when you log into Apigee,
at just apigee.com/edge,

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.146
in your SaaS environment, this
is what you are going to see.

00:28:24.146 --> 00:28:26.520
One of the first things, most
likely, you are going to do

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:30.000
is create an API proxy.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.240
So the proxy is
really the facade,

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:36.930
which is separating out your
backend from the frontend.

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:40.520
So over here, I have a
whole bunch of-- sorry--

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:44.290
I have a whole bunch of facade,
a whole bunch of proxies.

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:46.860
But I'm going to open up
this particular proxy.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:49.670
If you can see, it has
two different backends

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:50.910
which it talks to.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.560
So one is that legacy
backend, which I talked about,

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:55.640
which is the legacy Spring app.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:59.910
And the other is the
more modern application.

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:03.300
And both of these are exposed
as one single application.

00:29:03.300 --> 00:29:07.560
So the immediate beauty is
that, when a consumer is trying

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:11.550
to call-- they are going to be
called as endpoint /v1/hotels--

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:13.220
they don't know
whether, downstream, it

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:14.850
is going to the
legacy application

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:17.550
or to the modern
GKE-based application.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:21.480
That is something which Apigee
can take care of dynamically

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:25.680
and can route it to
different backends.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.380
So when you move on to this
particular screen, called

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:32.130
Develop, this is where it
becomes a lot more interesting,

00:29:32.130 --> 00:29:35.700
and you can actually see all
the different getallhotels,

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:38.110
getallhoteldetails, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:39.930
So these are the
different API calls,

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:43.350
which Apigee is configured
to process right now.

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:46.000
And I will not spend
a lot of time on this,

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.190
but this is what we call a
Proxy Editor or a Policy Editor,

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:51.150
where, using different
kinds of policies,

00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:53.100
you can configure
all sorts of things,

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:55.915
from put the right
amount of Quota

00:29:55.915 --> 00:29:58.740
or Spike Arrest or
caching or security,

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:02.450
and do orchestrations and
version management and things

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:02.950
like that.

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:10.120
Let me quickly jump onto
this screen called Trace.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:11.802
So Trace is a live debugger.

00:30:11.802 --> 00:30:14.010
If you have ever written a
code, you have most likely

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:17.070
used a debugger in your IDE.

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:19.800
So Trace is such a debugger.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.560
So this is where I have
a script running, which

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:24.390
is sending some API calls.

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:27.270
And I can see those API
calls actually coming in.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.310
And I can actually click
on a particular call.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:31.790
And I can see how
the call came in,

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:34.455
the different policies, which
it is invoking, like there it

00:30:34.455 --> 00:30:37.160
is invoking a policy
to verify API key,

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:39.670
a policy to remove
the quota policy.

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:41.140
There are some conditions.

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.260
And finally, the call
goes to the backend.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.880
I can actually,
then, go down and see

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:47.880
all the different parameters
that particular policy

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:49.300
affected.

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:50.970
So this is an
awesome way for you

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:55.050
to debug things in real life.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.370
And you can export
the session and can

00:30:57.370 --> 00:31:01.290
do an offline tracing later on.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:03.770
Now, let me move on to a
little bit different screen.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:08.420
So we created this UI, a
very simple hotels app.

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:10.700
If I click down below,
you will actually

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:14.150
see it has been configured
to use that v1/hotels,

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:16.172
and I'm passing an API key.

00:31:16.172 --> 00:31:20.840
So as part of the demo
setup, I created two apps.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:23.180
One is a legacy app,
and one is a modern app.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:26.840
Both of these apps calls
the same API, v1/hotels.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:31.880
Now, based on the API key, with
which the app is configured,

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:34.520
the call will go to
a different backend.

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:37.490
So Apigee is checking,
at that API key,

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:39.800
and is discerning which
one of the backends

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:41.990
this particular app
is configured to.

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:47.270
So if I actually go ahead and,
let's say, search for Seattle,

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:52.310
I will get all the details
from that backend application.

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:54.690
So this is a very,
very simple demo.

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:58.210
But what we are hoping
the key takeaway

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.760
is you can see the simplicity,
that you are exposing

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:04.350
one single interface to
all sorts of applications,

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:06.460
whether your legacy
applications, which

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:10.100
you are scared to touch, because
the original developer is

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.200
no longer with the company
for many, many years,

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:15.000
or to the modern Android apps.

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:16.910
All of them get
the same interface.

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:19.850
And then Apigee can
dynamically redirect them

00:32:19.850 --> 00:32:21.910
to different backends.

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:23.900
And you can do so
many other things,

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:27.350
like manage different versions,
manage different styles

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:30.940
of APIs, different security
capabilities of the APIs

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:31.440
and whatnot.

00:32:33.709 --> 00:32:36.000
One other thing, which I
wanted to show you guys today,

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:37.980
is the analytics capability.

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:43.190
Because, as you have more and
more different styles of API,

00:32:43.190 --> 00:32:46.470
a higher usage of those APIs,
larger number of developers

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:50.190
using those APIs, the analytics
becomes very critical.

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:53.010
Because you need to
understand, very precisely, how

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:57.900
each developer or how each
app is using those APIs,

00:32:57.900 --> 00:33:01.200
at what error rate, what
latency, what throughput,

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:04.380
what is a business
justification for an API,

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:06.100
and things like that.

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:09.130
So I have one analytics,
which is open,

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:11.800
which, over the last
one day, is showing me

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:15.060
what's my overall total
traffic, my success versus error

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:19.680
rate, my average response times,
my traffic by different proxy.

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:23.400
So it might be I have a user's
API and I have a catalog API.

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:24.960
How do I know which
API is getting

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:28.350
used more often and at what
error rate and what throughput

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:29.910
and what not?

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:32.310
I can look into things
like API monitoring,

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:37.830
which actually helps me
see what are the last five

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:41.700
hours of my latency,
or the error rate,

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:44.970
and what is the total
traffic for individual APIs.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:49.050
I can actually go in and
can drill down and see, OK,

00:33:49.050 --> 00:33:51.240
I can configure
thresholds that, hey, I

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:56.270
want to get alerted
every time the throughput

00:33:56.270 --> 00:34:00.820
or, let's say, the error rate
goes beyond 0.1% and things

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:01.320
like that.

00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:04.050
It looks like I have
a little less data

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:05.560
than what I anticipated.

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:08.639
So we'll move on to some of
the other analytics, things

00:34:08.639 --> 00:34:11.760
like developer engagement, which
is really popular with product

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.110
managers.

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:16.219
Here, you can see, I can look at
all the different developers--

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:19.469
the details like
proxy name and things

00:34:19.469 --> 00:34:23.750
like that, then what
apps they are using.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:25.650
I can then drill
down and analyze

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.440
individual applications.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.230
So I can say, hey,
this developer

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:32.969
is making a lot of calls
from their Android app.

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:36.239
And I want to see
how their pattern is

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:40.230
different from last seven
days versus this seven days

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.499
and things like that.

00:34:42.499 --> 00:34:44.290
There are a whole bunch
of other analytics,

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:46.690
like error code
analytics or Geomap,

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:50.350
which helps you to get
an awesome overview

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.929
of analytics data,
or the traffic coming

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:54.544
from all around the world.

00:34:54.544 --> 00:34:55.960
And you can see
that, immediately,

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:57.400
calls coming from
Virginia, and I

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:59.740
can drill down and see where,
in Virginia, the call is

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:00.940
coming in from.

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:04.907
You can see your traffic, error
rate, average response times,

00:35:04.907 --> 00:35:06.490
and all those things,
and how are they

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:10.060
distributed over the world.

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:11.590
So I'm going to pause there.

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:13.480
This is just-- please
keep in mind this

00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:17.080
is just a very high-level
overview of Apigee,

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:19.570
and we have not even
touched probably

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:23.170
10% of the overall
platform capabilities.

00:35:23.170 --> 00:35:24.310
There's a lot more.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:26.710
Feel free to try out the
platform at Apigee.com.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:28.180
Just go and sign
up, and you should

00:35:28.180 --> 00:35:30.280
be able to get the
same interface as you

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:32.440
are seeing now.

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:35.230
And I'm going to pause there.

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:38.440
Please stay tuned for Q&amp;A. We
have a few questions coming in

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:41.320
during the whole session.

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:44.770
We'll be back in just
less than a minute.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:54.700
All right.

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:59.860
It looks like we got a few
questions during the session.

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:02.950
Let me try to address
them as best as I can.

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:06.070
The first question is, "Can I
use Apigee even if we are not

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:08.560
using Kubernetes or GKE?"

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:10.010
The answer is yes.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:14.270
So Apigee is very much
backend or platform agnostic.

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:17.230
Apigee only cares
about HTTP or HTTPS.

00:37:17.230 --> 00:37:21.370
So it doesn't matter
whether you are running GKE

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:23.694
or even if you're
running in GCP.

00:37:23.694 --> 00:37:25.360
You might be running
your private cloud,

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:28.480
your on-prem data centers,
might be Cloud Foundry,

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:29.760
or whatever it might be.

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:33.340
As long as that application
talks HTTP or HTTPS,

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:36.850
you can use Apigee
on top of that.

00:37:36.850 --> 00:37:39.190
And you can also
use other protocols

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:46.720
using custom connectors, using
things like TCP-based protocols

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:48.540
and things like that.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:52.180
So the answer is absolutely
yes, primarily HTTP and HTTPS,

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:56.330
and other protocols
using custom connectors

00:37:56.330 --> 00:37:58.510
The second question is,
"I don't have mobile apps.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:37:59.890
Is Apigee still relevant?"

00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:01.960
Absolutely.

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:04.120
Interestingly, you
will actually see--

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.400
traditionally, if you
look at how enterprises

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:11.830
have used Apigee, one of the
starting points, in many cases,

00:38:11.830 --> 00:38:16.900
is they use Apigee to streamline
their APIs internally,

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:19.240
irrespective of where
the mobile apps are,

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:22.480
whether you have mobile apps
or web apps or IoTs or whatnot.

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:24.670
Because a challenge,
which typically happens

00:38:24.670 --> 00:38:26.542
at almost every
enterprise of every size,

00:38:26.542 --> 00:38:28.750
is that, over the years,
people write different kinds

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:30.745
of APIs, different styles,
different versions.

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:37.150
How do you even get a good
control on those APIs?

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:38.890
Who are using those APIs?

00:38:38.890 --> 00:38:42.890
What is the usage pattern,
all that kind of stuff?

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:45.790
So yes, we have a
lot of customers who

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:48.610
use Apigee with mobile apps.

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:51.790
But who the consumer
is, that's kind

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:56.570
of not so much critical, as
long as a consumer talks APIs,

00:38:56.570 --> 00:39:00.260
or the consumer talks in HTTP
and HTTPS-based protocol.

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:04.080
So just to reiterate, the
answer is absolutely yes

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:05.890
for the second one.

00:39:05.890 --> 00:39:09.400
Third question, "Can I have
different security protocols

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:11.200
for my different APIs?"

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:14.560
This is actually becoming more
and more relevant and more

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:15.730
and more common.

00:39:15.730 --> 00:39:18.310
Because, as we see
enterprises, who

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:24.420
have started modernizing-- even
companies who are pretty new,

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:27.131
who have been in existence
even for like five years,

00:39:27.131 --> 00:39:28.630
they may have started
with something

00:39:28.630 --> 00:39:31.330
like OAuth and bearer tokens.

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:35.120
And now, you might have
evolved into JWTs, or OpenID

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:38.340
Connect and other
protocols, or maybe SPIFFE,

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:40.870
which I mentioned earlier.

00:39:40.870 --> 00:39:42.250
Obviously, other
enterprises, who

00:39:42.250 --> 00:39:43.624
have been there
much longer, they

00:39:43.624 --> 00:39:45.340
might have a lot
of older protocols,

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:49.430
like Kerberos or
SAML and whatnot.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:51.670
What you can do with Apigee
is you can do something

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:52.974
called credential mediation.

00:39:52.974 --> 00:39:54.890
What it means is, on the
southbound-- that is,

00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:56.300
Apigee to the backend--

00:39:56.300 --> 00:39:59.000
you will have these individual
different protocols.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:04.100
And the northbound side,
which is the client to Apigee,

00:40:04.100 --> 00:40:05.870
you can have one
standard protocol.

00:40:05.870 --> 00:40:08.180
Maybe you understand it as
an OAuth, maybe a couple

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:09.980
of different flows of OAuth.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.320
And you can have the
client talk to Apigee

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.840
using just those
two OAuth protocols.

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:16.490
And then Apigee,
on the southbound,

00:40:16.490 --> 00:40:21.860
will translate that to SAML or
JWT or OpenID Connect or TLS

00:40:21.860 --> 00:40:26.040
or whatever might be applicable.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:26.940
Great question.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:30.540
The next one, "Can Apigee
analytics data be exported?"

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:31.114
Yes.

00:40:31.114 --> 00:40:32.530
There are a few
different options.

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:35.560
So all the analytics
data which you saw--

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.950
and a lot of others,
which you did not--

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:40.050
you can export that to an API.

00:40:40.050 --> 00:40:42.300
You can do pretty
much a raw export,

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:44.760
or there is a pretty
powerful query syntax

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:49.260
through which you can export
just parts of that analytics

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:50.350
data.

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:53.790
And there is also
native integrations,

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:56.700
with things like BigQuery,
where you can directly

00:40:56.700 --> 00:40:58.920
export all the data to
BigQuery, so that you

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:00.850
do further analysis in GCP.

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:05.840
Next question, "How
can Apigee manage

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:07.820
different versions of my API?"

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.490
Again, great question.

00:41:09.490 --> 00:41:13.920
So there are two aspects
to that question.

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:16.980
One is how the platform
can do, and one

00:41:16.980 --> 00:41:19.290
is what are the best practices
of version management?

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:22.030
So let me address the
first question first.

00:41:22.030 --> 00:41:23.700
So you can manage--

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:25.740
for Apigee, different
versions of APIs

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:27.090
are just different APIs.

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:30.750
Whether you represent those
versions as query parameters

00:41:30.750 --> 00:41:32.920
or through headers or
through foreign parameters,

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:35.460
those are just
different endpoints.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:39.681
And Apigee just will
manage two different APIs.

00:41:39.681 --> 00:41:41.930
Apigee will also provide you
analytics out-of-the-box,

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:45.060
through which you can see
how the adoption of different

00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:46.330
versions are.

00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:51.050
And you can take
business decisions,

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:54.120
when to cut off a
specific version,

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:56.730
or which version is
getting adoption,

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:59.470
and how can you fine
tune and adjust those?

00:41:59.470 --> 00:42:03.510
Now, in terms of
best practices, we

00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:05.730
have seen a lot of
versioning over the years,

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:07.860
and we have best practices
on whether you should

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:11.430
do versioning through URLs
or through foreign params

00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:13.660
or through headers,
how it should

00:42:13.660 --> 00:42:16.830
vary based on the style of
APIs and things like that.

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:20.100
You can go over to Apigee
blog, and you can learn

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.380
a little bit more about those.

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:25.950
The final question, "Can
Apigee manage APIs of SaaS

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:27.930
applications we are consuming?"

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:28.770
Absolutely, yes.

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:31.980
The answer is very
similar to the first one.

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:33.630
As long as there
is an API, as long

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:36.210
as there is an HTTP or
HTTPS-based protocol,

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:40.110
absolutely, Apigee
can address those.

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:44.582
So with that, that's the
last question which we have.

00:42:44.582 --> 00:42:46.040
If there are any
follow-ups, we are

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:49.190
going to address those online.

00:42:49.190 --> 00:42:51.340
And stay tuned for
the next session,

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:54.380
"AutoML Vision, Making
Custom Image Analysis

00:42:54.380 --> 00:42:56.180
Possible for Every Business."

00:42:56.180 --> 00:42:57.950
Thank you, and have a great day.

00:42:57.950 --> 00:43:01.900
